A||||5||LarryB||Exhaust insulating wrap||||3||
Z||000000||LarryB||07-22-2001||10:00 PM||||Has anyone here tried using exhaust insulating wrap on their V6?  I was checking out their current prices on Magnecor sets at <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cyberauto.com</A>  when I came across their 'engine heat management products' page.<P>Seems like an interesting alternative to jet hot coating, just wondering if anyone here who has actually used it thought of it. ||||reg||3
Z||000001||California Kid||07-22-2001||10:11 PM||||It's great if you don't get the engine compartment wet !! Even at that, you take the risk of condensation making the wrap moist at cool down. Corrosion is the problem with tape wraps, that's the reason nearly everyone has switched to the hi tech ceramic coatings.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||07-23-2001||12:31 AM||||Never use header wrap. It will destroy your pipes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||07-23-2001||12:37 AM||||Fiero exaust is stainless, so i wouldnt be worried about the moisture.<BR>Has anyone tried fiberglass wrap? i bet it would allow the water to freely escape.  Hmm thinking about it Ive only seen tape and ceramic wraps for sale.  But i have a big roll of 1.5" wide fiberglass wrap.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Terrybogin||07-23-2001||12:42 AM||||One of my former jobs supplied me with a large quantity of steam pipe wrap. It was about the same as the "name brand" pipe wraps. I used it for years with not problems at all, even on standard mild steel headers.||||reg||1
Z||000005||West Coast Fiero||07-23-2001||04:57 PM||||fiberglass melts at about 250 degrees, average temp for exhaust is 900 to 1000 degrees, header wrap is a significant improvement, however it works great when kept up, but once its wet its all down hill from there||||reg||1
